carl sagan he has written so many  popular science books  and brought us the original cosmos  series which has inspired so many people  to dream about space the surface  of the earth is the shore of the cosmic  ocean  on this shore we've learned most of what  we know  recently we've waded a little way up  maybe  ankle deep and the water seems inviting  some part of our being knows this is  where we came from  we long to return and we can  because the cosmos is also within us  we're made of star stuff  we are a way for the cosmos to know  itself  sagan was also an accomplished  researcher  and is one of the most highly cited  planetary scientists  so i wanted to take a look today at  where that all started  by taking a look at his phd thesis  now sagan went to the university of  chicago  and he completed his phd in 1960  when he was age 25. the title  is physical studies of planets and his  supervisor  was gerard kuiper of whom the kuiper  belt is named after  on the cover page here we can see the  name carl sagan  and his signature the thesis is  in four parts part one  indigenous organic matter on the moon  part two biological contamination of the  moon  part three the radiation balance venus  and part 4 production of organic  molecules  and planetary atmospheres these topics  reflect his interest in astrobiology  he was known for thinking a lot about  life in the cosmos  and he was a big promoter of seti the  search for extraterrestrial intelligence  there is some chance that in the next  few decades we will get a  signal from some spectacularly distant  spectacularly exotic civilization and  everything on earth will as a  consequence change  if there's a plausible argument that  there isn't anybody out there  bearing in mind that we can be wrong we  ought to keep looking  because the question is of the most  supreme  importance it calibrates our place in  the universe  it tells us who we are and so  it is worthwhile trying to find other  civilizations  i would say no matter what part one  starts  off by saying that for some time after  its formation  the moon possessed an atmosphere  composed of  methane ammonia water vapor and hydrogen  and says the effect of solar uv  light on an atmosphere is that organic  molecules of fair complexity  are produced and the purpose of this  present paper  is to estimate the quantity of organic  matter  which formed in the primitive lunar  atmosphere  diffused to the surface and survived to  the present day  this was written 10 years before the  apollo astronauts would land on the moon  and sagan is pretty interested in the  idea of finding organic molecules there  organic molecules i guess are of special  interest  since they are precursors to life  or are used a lot in biology  you may have heard that the moon has  such a weak atmosphere now that it could  be considered to have no atmosphere  or actually be considered a pretty good  vacuum  but sagan is referencing material that  says that that wasn't always the case  the moon's atmosphere requires  replenishment from the interior of the  moon  and gradually escapes so the moon's  atmosphere might once have been  very thick before most of it has been  lost to space  sagan first works out a surface density  of synthesized material  so this would be a covering of these  organic molecules  and it's more common to hear of uv light  breaking down molecules  than helping to build them but remember  that sunlight is thought to be  an essential part of the equation for  how we got  life in the first place that the effect  of  uv radiation on bonds and molecules  helps them break down but also builds  into longer chains  the effect of heat and uv light on the  molecules  is remarkable he says a large fraction  of the molecules partake in organic  synthesis  of a higher order of complexity  polypeptides arise from amino acids  hydrocarbon diamonds form long chain  polymers  and in general very complex organic  molecules are constructed  this continued radiation and high  temperatures  would eventually destroy these molecules  but during this time  there were a lot of meteors hitting the  moon and a lot of this  meteorite material falling into the  lunar atmosphere  so sagan says the moon's surface must  have received a dust cover  probably composed primarily of silicates  and ices  the organic molecules would then be  covered  by a protective layer insulating them  from the extremes of temperature  and absorbing the radiation so he says  that provided no large-scale destructive  events  we may anticipate the presence of  complex  organic matter beneath the moon's dust  layer  this would only apply to regions that  have had no extensive lava flows  the southern highland appears to be such  a region  as does much of the far side of the moon  and he finishes this first section of  the thesis by saying that  to figure out much more what we'll need  is a  sample of lunar material from below the  surface  scientists have since found some of  these molecules  in samples that we took from the moon  and although we haven't found  any life there we have found some of the  essential ingredients  part two is about the biological  contamination of the moon  sagan is expecting that soon humans will  start landing on the moon  and he expresses concern that  terrestrial microorganisms  meaning from earth and organic matter  deposited with the packages may obscure  detection of  or interact with possible organisms or  organic matter indigenous to the moon  such biological contamination of the  moon  would represent an unparalleled  scientific disaster  and because of it scientific  opportunities lost on the moon  may not be recoupable elsewhere  he lists four classes of possible  lunar biological contamination and i  haven't found reference to these words  anywhere outside this thesis  so maybe they are words of his own  invention  but the first one is bio mixi which is  that the moon may contain  no indigenous living organisms and may  be  incapable of supporting terrestrial  organisms  however there may be relics of primitive  indigenous organisms  deposited on or beneath the surface  and a vehicle impacting the moon may  distribute its contents  over the surface such that further  investigations  might be unable to distinguish between  the primitive indigenous organisms  and ones that came along from earth this  is pretty similar to the second type  listed as sapro-mixie and then we have  the third type  phagomixie which is that the moon may  contain  no indigenous living organisms but may  be capable of supporting  some terrestrial organisms this would  require subsurface organic matter  moisture and heat sources the  possibility then exists that  deposited microorganisms from earth in  the absence of  any competitors or predators will  multiply  at a geometric rate limited only by  the availability of water and  metabolites  this would give a biological explosion  and you can probably see that this  wouldn't be  very helpful for trying to study the  history of the moon  and the fourth type of contamination is  eco  mixy that the moon itself may contain  indigenous living organisms that is that  there may be life  on the moon there is then the  possibility that deposited  microorganisms from earth  by competition with or parotism upon  even one species of lung organism will  disrupt the ecology  completely so these are kind of all the  ways we could screw things up on the  moon  by accidentally depositing some of our  own biology  and doing a bit of math and looking at a  few of the numbers  including the survival law which would  tell how long the organisms from earth  could even survive if they were  accidentally left on the moon  sagan comes up with a few conclusions  about  how likely each of these possibilities  are  he finds that most microorganisms would  be killed by the uv radiation  within a few hours but if any make their  way  into a lunar crevasse or are otherwise  shielded from the radiation  then they might survive for quite some  time  and while the possibilities may be low  of  life spreading or surviving or even  getting there in the first place  sagan finds that all of those types of  contamination  cannot be excluded and are not  negligible  and i suppose the whole paper is a  little bit of a warning to make sure  that when spacecraft  leave earth on their way to the moon  that we sterilize them really thoroughly  the third part of the thesis the  radiation balance of venus  is the longest section and probably  contains the most  notable work sagan was trying to explain  a recent microwave observation of venus  that gave its surface temperature as  being near  600 kelvin previously scientists had  taken what they thought they knew about  the surface of venus  and used that to try and work out a  projected temperature  but sagan worked backwards he took this  temperature measurement and tried to  figure out how it came about  it was certainly higher than the  expected temperature of such a planet  which is quoted here as being no more  than 350 degrees kelvin  and what sagan says here is that it is  clear that a surface temperature of  600 kelvin demands a very  efficient greenhouse effect so his  calculations found that essentially a  runaway  greenhouse effect was able to explain  this very high temperature  this effect would occur when there are a  lot of greenhouse gases in the  atmosphere  trapping radiation from the sun and  heating up the planet  carbon dioxide and water vapor are both  greenhouse gases and sagan proposes that  it is water excess water in the  atmosphere that could explain the  greenhouse effect  on venus but later missions to venus  actually found that there was very  little water in the atmosphere  and instead it was almost entirely  carbon dioxide  the radiation balance of a planet is a  very delicate  thing and it seems likely that venus  wasn't always  so hot and unwelcoming it might have  been much more livable like earth  but it is closer to the sun and hotter  and drier than earth  and the carbon compounds might have  evaporated  rather than remaining in the rocks  leading to its greenhouse effect to  essentially run away  leading to a vicious cycle where the  planet just gets hotter and hotter  sagan discusses what might have happened  if the earth had been placed in the  orbit of venus  in its primitive times and essentially  its  fate is much the same the oceans would  boil  the carbon dioxide abundance would go up  and earth wouldn't be the home  that we know it to be all of this  research into what  caused the fate of venus and thinking  about what might happen to earth in  different circumstances  probably led carl sagan to be quite  worried about things like global warming  and passionate about trying to  communicate what was going on  now no one has proposed that the trouble  with venus is that there once was  venusians who drove fuel and efficient  cars  but our nearest neighbor nevertheless is  a stark warning  on the possible fate of an earth-like  world  the last section of the thesis is this  one here  which is actually an experimental  project sagan is again  talking about how organic molecules are  produced  when energy is supplied to an atmosphere  of methane ammonia water and hydrogen  he uses spectroscopy to find the spectra  of some of these known end products  and list the features that he finds in  this table  what he then does is compares this  laboratory spectra  to spectra taken with a telescope but  looking at some of the jovian planets  including jupiter and saturn from his  observations he thinks that  it's possible that there is hydrogen  cyanide  present in the atmosphere of jupiter  however this was only a preliminary  report  and so he's not able to really  conclusively say these things  but has set up this field of science for  some further investigation  and that is a look at carl sagan's  thesis which would set him  up for a very successful career further  tackling all of these questions  thinking about the atmospheres of  planets and the likelihood of finding  life there  thanks for watching and thanks as always  to my patreon supporters  who make these videos possible a special  shout out to today's patreon cats of the  day  minerva and isis i hope to see you next  [Music]  time  you
 

